{
 "_about": "Each unit: the submodel of JappaWakka's recreations it is rendered from (model), the game's sprite name (sprite), the suffix of its standing sprite per direction (still), and where it can be: land, water or both (amphibious units also get partly submerged .water. sprites). studs: 'none' (the default: the game's units have flat-topped bricks) or 'full' (its pickup brick piles show their studs; the buildings' map sprites do not, their portraits do). single: one sprite, not four directions (the pickups); colours: rendered once per colour, the model's yellow (LDraw 14) recoloured to each LDraw colour, {colour} in the sprite name. hero_model: the info panel's portrait is of another build than the map's (the game's own inconsistency: JappaWakka modelled it too); hero_as: the game reuses another unit's portrait. Rendering with --consistent-heroes makes every portrait from the map's model instead. model_file: a model of this project's own (extra/), not JappaWakka's. flies: the unit hovers this many pixels over its tile on the map, a dithered shadow under it (its sprite's registration point says where the ground is: out/<unit>/sprites.json). replace_parts: parts of the model's own (its top level) swapped for others, where the recreation takes a liberty with the game's build (the Gas Station's and Marina's tiled bases are studded plates in the game). hero_rig_from: a unit with no portrait in the game lit as the portrait of this one is (wbrender/heronormal.py). consistent_like: the game's portrait a consistent portrait (one rendered from the map's model) stands for -- its camera and light (the dirtbuggy's map model is the buggy's portrait build, yellow); hero_as serves when not given. like: a unit with no sprites in the game, framed as this one is -- its map camera (turns, height, skew, size) and its portrait's camera. turn: degrees added to the borrowed cameras' turns, for a model built facing otherwise (our airport models face away from JappaWakka's buildings). size: the borrowed map camera's scale multiplied, to bring the sprite to the area of the one it is framed as (the game's buildings each fill about a tile, whatever their bricks).",
